**Theorem 13.2 (one oriented hemisphere for every residual contact).**
In addition to the hypotheses of Theorem 13.1, suppose

$$
a_o^2+b_o^2>0,\qquad w_o^TR_+w_o\ge0.
\tag{FC.46}
$$

Then every nonmerged saturated contact has the same sign of $u$,
strictly positive height, and $L_o(x)<0$. The conclusion permits
$w_o^TR_+w_o=0$ and an empty residual set.

Proof. If $\mathcal I_-$ is empty there is nothing to prove.
Otherwise $a_o+b_oz$ has no zero in this interval. For $b_o=0$
it is the nonzero constant $a_o$. For $b_o\ne0$, a zero
$z_0=-a_o/b_o$ inside would imply

$$
w_o^TR_+w_o=b_o^2r_Q(z_0)<0,
\tag{FC.47}
$$

contrary to (FC.46). Continuity on one interval therefore gives a
fixed strict sign of $a_o+b_oz$. Every residual contact satisfies
$L_o(x)=u(a_o+b_oz)\le0$ by (FC.43), and neither factor can
vanish. Hence $L_o(x)<0$ and all such $u$ have the same sign.
These are directed-circle contacts: no quotient by antipodes has
been taken. Equality in (FC.46) excludes an interior zero just as
strict positivity does, because the cone itself is open.
$\square$

